  "note": "Devanagari to Limbu (Sirijanga). Most Limbu speakers in Nepal are schooled in Devanagari and write Limbu in the LIFWBT-style Devanagari orthography, so this lets someone write in the script they already know and obtain the script of their heritage. The correspondence follows that orthography as documented in the project's own Limbu G2P: DEVANAGARI LETTER GLOTTAL STOP becomes mukphreng, the visarga used for vowel length becomes kemphreng, a coda written consonant + virama becomes the dedicated small letter, and a nukta on a mid vowel sign lowers it to the Limbu sign carrying that value. The Limbu sign names are counterintuitive and the G2P is the authority: VOWEL SIGN EE is /e/ while VOWEL SIGN E is /ɛ/, and OO is /o/ while O is /ɔ/, so mapping by name similarity would swap the mid vowels. Limbu has no retroflex series, so the Devanagari retroflexes fold onto the dentals exactly as the G2P reads them; that is lossy and inherent to the target script. The danda and double danda are left unmapped because Limbu does not share them.",
